{"id":"coding-variables","title":"Coding: variables and data types","subject":"Coding","label":"Coding","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":171,"duration":171.48,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":458,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Everyday puzzle","start":0,"end":13.275},{"id":"explain","title":"Variables","start":13.275,"end":49.735},{"id":"explain2","title":"Data types","start":49.735,"end":87.495},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":87.495,"end":110.975},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":110.975,"end":120.815},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":120.815,"end":126.785},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":126.785,"end":146.725},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":146.725,"end":171.48}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":6.72,"text":"Imagine a labelled box where you keep your score in a game. You write a name on it, then put a number inside.","w":[0.58,1.06,1.16,1.54,1.98,2.14,2.3,2.5,2.68,3.02,3.12,3.2,4.02,4.18,4.38,4.46,4.74,4.86,5.42,5.68,5.86,5.94,6.28]},{"s":0,"a":7.02,"b":12.85,"text":"I'm Kora, and today we'll see how code uses boxes like that. That simple idea is a variable.","w":[7.12,7.28,8.02,8.14,8.42,8.62,8.8,9,9.32,9.64,10.12,10.32,11.06,11.32,11.7,12.12,12.26,12.32]},{"s":1,"a":13.7,"b":20.86,"text":"A variable is a named place that stores a value. Think of a labelled box: the name helps you find what is inside.","w":[13.76,13.84,14.48,14.64,14.74,15.12,15.48,15.68,16.04,16.1,17.02,17.26,17.38,17.48,17.9,18.82,19,19.32,19.56,19.74,20.04,20.22,20.36]},{"s":1,"a":21.16,"b":27.55,"text":"In Python, you assign with the equals sign. Here it means store this value, not is equal to as in maths.","w":[21.24,21.36,21.86,22.04,22.5,22.66,22.86,23.2,24.06,24.32,24.4,24.72,25.1,25.32,25.76,26.02,26.2,26.5,26.82,26.98,27.08]},{"s":1,"a":27.85,"b":32.88,"text":"For example, age equals sixteen stores the number sixteen under the name age.","w":[27.88,28.06,29.04,29.38,29.92,30.44,30.9,31.04,31.32,31.9,32.08,32.22,32.56]},{"s":1,"a":33.18,"b":40.6,"text":"So score equals ten stores ten under the name score. The value can change later; that is reassignment.","w":[33.28,33.7,34.22,34.66,34.96,35.62,36.12,36.32,36.44,36.74,37.84,38,38.36,38.54,38.86,39.56,39.76,39.92]},{"s":1,"a":40.9,"b":44.78,"text":"Later, age equals seventeen replaces the old value with a new one.","w":[40.94,41.48,41.72,42.02,42.84,43.38,43.58,43.78,44.18,44.36,44.42,44.66]},{"s":1,"a":45.08,"b":49.31,"text":"Choose names that say what is stored, like score, not just s.","w":[45.16,45.5,45.84,46.06,46.3,46.52,46.66,47.5,47.74,48.44,48.64,49.08]},{"s":2,"a":50.16,"b":54.67,"text":"Every value has a data type. An integer is a whole number, like seven.","w":[50.24,50.52,50.96,51.16,51.24,51.56,52.36,52.56,53.04,53.16,53.24,53.48,54.02,54.32]},{"s":2,"a":54.97,"b":60.86,"text":"A float has a decimal point, like three point five. A string is text, written in quotes.","w":[55.06,55.18,55.52,55.74,55.84,56.28,57.04,57.26,57.52,57.8,58.62,58.7,59.06,59.2,59.92,60.2,60.34]},{"s":2,"a":61.16,"b":66.96,"text":"A boolean is either True or False, like a yes or no. The type decides what you can do.","w":[61.22,61.32,61.88,62.04,62.34,62.76,62.92,63.66,63.86,63.94,64.24,64.38,65.4,65.54,65.82,66.24,66.4,66.54,66.7]},{"s":2,"a":67.26,"b":72.78,"text":"Numbers can be integers or floats. Text is a string, and truth values are booleans.","w":[67.34,67.74,67.92,68.12,68.68,68.84,69.68,70.08,70.22,70.32,71.02,71.18,71.52,72.04,72.18]},{"s":2,"a":73.08,"b":78.19,"text":"Use quotes for a string, like the word hello. Adding two numbers gives a sum.","w":[73.24,73.5,73.94,74.12,74.2,74.88,75.1,75.22,75.52,76.5,76.8,77.06,77.48,77.74,77.86]},{"s":2,"a":78.49,"b":83.06,"text":"Adding two strings joins them. The string five and the number five are different.","w":[78.6,78.9,79.16,79.62,79.94,80.7,80.84,81.16,81.6,81.7,81.84,82.12,82.46,82.62]},{"s":2,"a":83.36,"b":87.07,"text":"String five plus string five gives five five as text, not ten.","w":[83.46,83.82,84.18,84.44,84.74,85.08,85.32,85.62,85.88,85.98,86.56,86.76]},{"s":3,"a":87.92,"b":93.95,"text":"Let price equal two hundred and quantity equal three. Total equals price times quantity.","w":[87.96,88.16,88.56,88.86,89.1,89.52,89.7,90.42,90.7,91.72,92.14,92.54,92.88,93.36]},{"s":3,"a":94.25,"b":98.14,"text":"So total is two hundred times three. Print total gives six hundred.","w":[94.32,94.54,94.94,95.14,95.34,95.64,95.98,96.76,97,97.32,97.6,97.82]},{"s":3,"a":98.44,"b":103.77,"text":"That is six hundred, a number, not text. The board shows the real Python code.","w":[98.46,98.66,98.86,99.12,99.86,99.98,100.76,101,101.84,101.98,102.28,102.56,102.7,103.02,103.46]},{"s":3,"a":104.07,"b":110.55,"text":"If we later change price, total stays six hundred until we calculate it again. Python does not update it for us.","w":[104.1,104.22,104.36,104.64,104.94,105.58,105.92,106.24,106.52,106.88,107.14,107.28,107.72,107.84,108.7,109.18,109.38,109.66,109.98,110.08,110.28]},{"s":4,"a":111.4,"b":116.69,"text":"Quick check. Suppose x equals five. Then x equals x plus two.","w":[111.44,111.72,112.56,113.24,113.56,113.9,114.7,115.16,115.46,115.98,116.14,116.42]},{"s":4,"a":116.99,"b":117.94,"text":"What is x now?","w":[117.04,117.24,117.44,117.62]},{"s":4,"a":118.39,"b":120.39,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[118.4,118.74,119,119.12,119.4,119.86,120.04]},{"s":5,"a":121.24,"b":126.36,"text":"x is seven. Because x plus two means five plus two, and that is seven.","w":[121.38,121.66,121.84,122.5,122.98,123.22,123.54,123.86,124.18,124.54,124.82,125.52,125.66,125.88,126.06]},{"s":6,"a":127.21,"b":132.18,"text":"First, don't read the equals sign as a maths statement. It stores the value on the right.","w":[127.3,128,128.26,128.46,128.62,128.96,129.36,129.52,129.6,129.92,130.74,130.9,131.24,131.34,131.72,131.82,131.94]},{"s":6,"a":132.48,"b":139.57,"text":"Second, don't put numbers in quotes and then do arithmetic. Quotes make text, so addition joins instead.","w":[132.6,133.48,133.72,133.96,134.3,134.46,135,135.14,135.34,135.52,136.7,137.08,137.32,138.08,138.28,138.72,139.1]},{"s":6,"a":139.87,"b":146.3,"text":"Third, give a variable a value before you use it, and avoid vague names. Always assign before use.","w":[139.92,140.62,140.82,140.9,141.42,141.52,141.94,142.28,142.5,142.7,143.12,143.26,143.7,144.02,144.74,145.14,145.54,146]},{"s":7,"a":147.15,"b":150.76,"text":"Remember: a variable is a named place that stores a value.","w":[147.22,148.12,148.2,148.82,148.96,149.06,149.38,149.72,149.92,150.26,150.32]},{"s":7,"a":151.06,"b":154.18,"text":"The equals sign assigns, and the value can change.","w":[151.06,151.24,151.62,151.96,152.88,152.98,153.12,153.52,153.72]},{"s":7,"a":154.48,"b":161.71,"text":"Data types include integer, float, string and boolean, and the type controls what operations make sense.","w":[154.48,154.84,155.26,155.82,156.54,157.24,157.78,157.92,159.02,159.16,159.3,159.6,160.12,160.4,161,161.24]},{"s":7,"a":162.01,"b":166.58,"text":"Top tip: name variables clearly and check the type before you calculate.","w":[162.06,162.38,163.28,163.6,164.2,164.8,165,165.28,165.4,165.68,165.98,166.12]},{"s":7,"a":166.88,"b":170.88,"text":"Keep practising with Knowzone, and these small steps will soon feel natural.","w":[166.9,167.18,167.72,167.9,168.46,168.6,168.8,169.18,169.58,169.82,170.16,170.46]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Everyday puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Labelled box: write a name, put a number inside"},{"at":6.92,"kind":"text","text":"Code uses boxes like that: variables"}]},{"at":13.45,"heading":"Variables","items":[{"at":13.6,"kind":"term","text":"variable","detail":"A named place that stores a value"},{"at":21.06,"kind":"term","text":"assign","detail":"The equals sign stores this value, not maths equality"},{"at":27.75,"kind":"formula","text":"age = 16"}]},{"at":32.93,"heading":"Reassignment","items":[{"at":33.08,"kind":"formula","text":"score = 10"},{"at":33.08,"kind":"term","text":"reassignment","detail":"The value stored can change later"},{"at":40.8,"kind":"formula","text":"age = 17"},{"at":44.98,"kind":"tip","text":"Choose names that say what is stored, e.g. score not s"}]},{"at":49.91,"heading":"Data types","items":[{"at":50.06,"kind":"term","text":"integer","detail":"A whole number, e.g. 7"},{"at":54.87,"kind":"term","text":"float","detail":"A number with a decimal point, e.g. 3.5"},{"at":54.87,"kind":"term","text":"string","detail":"Text written in quotes"},{"at":61.06,"kind":"term","text":"boolean","detail":"Either True or False (yes/no)"},{"at":61.06,"kind":"text","text":"The type decides what you can do."}]},{"at":67.01,"heading":"Types in practice","items":[{"at":67.16,"kind":"text","text":"Numbers: integer/float; text: string; truth: boolean"},{"at":72.98,"kind":"text","text":"Use quotes for a string, e.g. \"hello\""},{"at":72.98,"kind":"text","text":"Adding two numbers gives a sum"},{"at":78.39,"kind":"text","text":"Adding strings joins them; string \"5\" is not number 5"},{"at":83.26,"kind":"text","text":"\"5\" + \"5\" = \"55\" (text, not 10)"}]},{"at":87.67,"heading":"Worked example","items":[{"at":87.82,"kind":"formula","text":"price = 200"},{"at":87.82,"kind":"formula","text":"quantity = 3"},{"at":87.82,"kind":"formula","text":"total = price * quantity"},{"at":94.15,"kind":"formula","text":"200 × 3 = 600"},{"at":94.15,"kind":"text","text":"print(total) → 600"}]},{"at":98.19,"heading":"Worked example: result","items":[{"at":98.34,"kind":"text","text":"600 is a number, not text"},{"at":103.97,"kind":"text","text":"Change price later → total stays 600 until recalculated"},{"at":103.97,"kind":"text","text":"Run total = price * quantity again to update"}]},{"at":110.97,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":110.97,"kind":"question","text":"Suppose x = 5. Then x = x + 2. What is x now?"}]},{"at":120.99,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":121.14,"kind":"formula","text":"x = 5 + 2 = 7"},{"at":121.14,"kind":"text","text":"x is 7"}]},{"at":126.96,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":127.11,"kind":"wrong","text":"Read = as a maths equality"},{"at":127.11,"kind":"right","text":"= stores the value on the right"},{"at":132.38,"kind":"wrong","text":"Put numbers in quotes, then add"},{"at":132.38,"kind":"right","text":"Quotes make text; addition joins"},{"at":139.77,"kind":"wrong","text":"Use a variable before assigning it"}]},{"at":146.9,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":147.05,"kind":"term","text":"variable","detail":"A named place that stores a value"},{"at":150.96,"kind":"text","text":"= assigns; the value can change"},{"at":154.38,"kind":"text","text":"Types: integer, float, string, boolean"},{"at":161.91,"kind":"tip","text":"Name variables clearly; check the type before calculating"},{"at":166.78,"kind":"text","text":"Keep practising with Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":120.64,"seconds":10,"question":"Suppose x = 5. Then x = x + 2. What is x now?","answer":"x = 7, because x + 2 means 5 + 2 = 7."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}